          <!-- JOB MARKET PAPER -->

          <article class="research-paper featured">

            <div class="paper-status">
              Job Market Paper · Dissertation chapter 3
            </div>

            <h2>
              The Surveillant Reflex: Political Threat and the Market
              Expansion of Home Surveillance
            </h2>

            <p class="paper-description">
              How does political conflict shape consumer demand for surveillance technologies? Using purchasing histories from a 
              nationally representative consumer panel, I estimate the effects of nearby Black Lives Matter (BLM) protests on the adoption of home surveillance 
              devices. I find that proximity to sustained BLM protest activity increases the hazard of adopting home surveillance devices, 
              but that this effect is considerably stronger in Republican counties. 
              I theorize this pattern as <strong> protective polarization</strong>, where partisan identity 
              shapes the interpretation of a protest as reform or threat. 
              In this case, BLM protests generated consumer demand for monitoring technologies among politically opposed populations. 
              Social movements can create demand in consumer markets not only by mobilizing sympathetic consumers but also by 
              provoking protective responses among those who view the movement as a threat. The adoption of these technologies 
              may further entrench surveillance in private everyday life, leading to more personal monitoring and diminished privacy.               
            </p>

          </article>

          <!-- PAPER -->

          <article class="research-paper">

            <div class="paper-status">
              Work in progress · Dissertation chapter 1
            </div>

            <h2>
              Digital Tracking Technologies and Organizational Strategy
              in the Field of Higher Education
            </h2>

            <p class="paper-description">
              Organizations vary in how they collect information about online users. 
              This paper asks why organizations develop different approaches to digital observation. 
              I trace how colleges and universities adopt digital tracking technologies to collect 
              behavioral information from website users. 
              Drawing on longitudinal observations of university homepages, I model the organizational determinants of different data collection strategies and show 
              how organizations expand, modify, and withdraw their digital observation practices.
            </p>

          </article>

