          <article class="research-paper">

            <div class="paper-status">
              Working Paper · Preparing for submission
            </div>

            <h2>
              Digital Redlining in College Recruitment: 
              How Search Location Shapes Access to Information
            </h2>

            <p class="paper-authors">
              Carson W. Phillips & Lincoln Quillian
            </p>

            <p class="paper-description">

              Many students begin their college search online, yet the information they encounter is not uniform. 
              Search results are personalized based on users’ locations and other digital trace data, 
              potentially shaping access to educational opportunities. 
              We examine how the characteristics of the ZIP code from which a search originates 
              affect the college information returned by search engines. 
              We conduct a digital audit experiment and scrape Google search results for “colleges" while 
              systematically manipulating browser parameters to simulate searches originating from different ZIP codes. 
              We repeatedly conduct searches across all ZIP codes in the United States. 
              We find that ZIP code income is a strong predictor of the types of institutions displayed. 
              Searches originating in lower-income ZIP codes disproportionately feature colleges with lower completion rates, 
              lower post-graduate earnings, and higher post-graduate default rates than searches originating in more affluent ZIP codes. 
              We identify potential mechanisms underlying these disparities across organic and sponsored search results. 
              The characteristics of nearby colleges are a key driver of inequality in organic results, 
              while sponsored results provide evidence that for-profit and online colleges target lower-income ZIP codes. 
              We discuss the implications of differential search results for inequality in college outcomes.
            </p>

          </article>



